# Agent Clients

An Agent Client connects Pontia to a coding agent's native session, control channel, and conversation history. The agent runs locally with its own credentials, configuration, tools, and filesystem permissions.

## Bring your own subscription

Pontia runs supported official CLI processes—and Claude Code through its Agent SDK—directly on your machine. It uses each client's existing sign-in and provider configuration without extracting or forwarding OAuth tokens. You can keep using subscriptions such as Claude, ChatGPT/Codex, Grok, Kiro, and Antigravity, plus Pi provider credentials, API keys, and custom model endpoints configured in the native client.

## Agent capability matrix

| Client | Connection | Session controls | Approval modes | Model and reasoning | Directory isolation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Pi](/docs/agent-clients/pi) | Native TUI plugin | Resume, steer, interrupt, timeline branches | Default, restricted, YOLO | Client catalog and advertised effort levels | Workspace per Session; Git worktree per parallel write task |
| [Codex](/docs/agent-clients/codex) | Native app-server | Resume, steer, interrupt, edit an earlier prompt into a new Session | Default, restricted, YOLO | Client catalog and advertised effort levels | Workspace per Session; Git worktree per parallel write task |
| [Claude Code](/docs/agent-clients/claude) | Claude Agent SDK | Resume, steer, interrupt, edit an earlier prompt into a new Session | Default, restricted, YOLO | Client catalog and advertised effort levels | Workspace per Session; Git worktree per parallel write task |
| [Grok Build](/docs/agent-clients/grok) | ACP server | Resume, interject, interrupt, compact when advertised | Default, restricted, YOLO | ACP catalog and advertised effort levels | Workspace per Session; Git worktree per parallel write task |
| [Google Antigravity](/docs/agent-clients/antigravity) | ACP server | Resume, steer, interrupt | Default, restricted, YOLO | ACP catalog and advertised effort levels | Workspace per Session; Git worktree per parallel write task |
| [Kiro](/docs/agent-clients/kiro) | ACP server | Resume, steer and compact when advertised, interrupt | Default, restricted, YOLO | ACP catalog and advertised effort levels | Workspace per Session; Git worktree per parallel write task |

## Run parallel tasks in Git worktrees

A Lead Bot can delegate independent tasks to parallel Helpers. Use [Agent Profiles](/docs/agent-profiles) to select the client, model, reasoning effort, permissions, and instructions for each role.

For concurrent changes to one repository, start the Helpers in the original active Workspace and ask each one to create a short-lived branch in its own Git worktree. The worktrees remain associated with that Workspace, so every Helper keeps the same project context while editing a separate checkout. Open a Helper's native Session to inspect its work and reported diffs, and use the Dashboard's Files and Git panels to review the active Workspace. The Lead Bot can coordinate validation and integration as the branches become ready.

| Client | One-step installer | Authentication |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Pi](/docs/agent-clients/pi) | `pontia client install pi` | Pi provider credentials on the device |
| [Codex](/docs/agent-clients/codex) | `pontia client install codex` | ChatGPT Device Code in the Dashboard, or Codex credentials on the device |
| [Claude Code](/docs/agent-clients/claude) | `pontia client install claude-agent` | Claude Code credentials on the device |
| [Google Antigravity](/docs/agent-clients/antigravity) | `pontia client install antigravity` | Antigravity sign-in on the device |
| [Grok Build](/docs/agent-clients/grok) | `pontia client install grok` | Grok Device Code in the Dashboard |
| [Kiro](/docs/agent-clients/kiro) | `pontia client install kiro` | Kiro Free or Pro Device Code in the Dashboard |

Grok Build, Antigravity, and Kiro can run ordinary Sessions, Bots, helpers, memory tasks, and Workflow nodes. Their individual guides cover models, remote controls, and execution modes.

## Install and enable a Client

Open **Settings → Agent Clients** in the Dashboard, choose a Client, and select **Install**. Pontia shows the planned source, destination, and prerequisites, runs the installer on the device, applies its local integration setup, and enables the Client for new Sessions.

The same flow is available from a terminal:

```sh
pontia client install CLIENT
```

Use `pontia client install --list` to inspect the registered installers and prerequisites. The installer asks for confirmation; add `--yes` for an unattended terminal. Pontia uses managed npm installs for Pi and Codex, the official Claude Code installer, and the official installers for Antigravity, Grok Build, and Kiro.

## Sign in from any browser

The Dashboard can start remote-compatible Device Code login on the selected device for Codex, Grok Build, and Kiro.

1. Open **Settings → Agent Clients** and select the Client.
2. Choose **Sign in**. For Kiro, select Free with AWS Builder ID or Pro with IAM Identity Center; the Pro flow also asks for the identity-provider URL and region.
3. Open the displayed verification page in any browser and enter the one-time code.
4. Return after the Dashboard confirms the signed-in account, then enable the Client.

Pi and Claude Code use credentials configured on the device, and Antigravity uses its local sign-in. Their Dashboard cards show the detected authentication state and relevant terminal command.

## Choose Clients during initialization

Run `pontia init` to select and check Pi, Codex, and Claude Code while initializing Pontia. Pi is selected by default, and the selected Clients are enabled for new Sessions.

Run `pontia setup pi`, `pontia setup codex`, or `pontia setup claude` to apply or repair a recommended local integration. Omit the Client to select interactively. In the Dashboard, **Initialize** runs the same setup and keeps its latest output available after reconnecting.

<h2 id="client-credentials-and-permissions">Choose Session permissions</h2>

Each Client uses its own credentials, sandbox, and local permissions. The optional `mode` field on `POST /api/v1/sessions` selects the execution mode; restarts, resumes, and history forks inherit it.

| Mode | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `default` | Use the Client's usual settings, as when starting a Session from the Dashboard |
| `yolo` | Run Pi, Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, Antigravity, or Kiro with approval-free tool use |
| `restricted` | Use the Client's built-in unattended tool policy; Bots, memory retrieval, and Dreaming use this mode |

The Dashboard presents the live controls offered by the selected Client, including interruption, history branching, and queued delivery. A Pontia Session keeps its client-native conversation identity, so reopening that native conversation continues the same Session.

Use [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) to restore Client discovery, startup, and output reporting.
